<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="title">The Lions of the Lord</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"0","bottom":"0"}}},"fontFamily":"montserrat"} -->
<p class="has-montserrat-font-family" id="author" style="margin-top:0;margin-bottom:0"><i>by Kyle Lehman</i></p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>The lion roars. His voice rides up the clouds;<br>And in Your open ears the tones bounce round.<br>You bend Your back in answer to the sound,<br>And comb the desert cliffs with silent teeth;<br>Through home and empty street pass padded feet.<br>You gather for the beast his tender meat.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Full length You lay him. Then You wind his springs<br>Through twitching tail and gathered golden hip,<br>From half hung jaw, bent eye, and trembling lip,<br>Claw clings, till tingling tension sings, out slings –<br>His terror galls and grips all creeping things.<br>Ah! Yours he is, and Yours the hand outstretched;<br>Yes, Yours the paw, the fallen prey out breath’d.<br>You own the subjects as You own the kings.<br><br>For once in Babylon the lions roared<br>Their hungry voices bounced through bars unheard<br>It seemed; for evil turned him round and stirred.<br>String stretched in silence, sleep was sought in vain.<br>Against the king, his subjects planned and played,<br>Yet down in Babylon a humble human prayed.<br><br>Injustice wrote the law and set the stone;<br>Cold courts conspired to feed the mice of men.<br>The lions roared, none answered them, but then –<br>As Daniel fell, one shout rang from Your throne,<br>“Touch not the sweating flesh upon his bone,<br>He is my own. As you, he does My will<br>Alone. He is not food; crush not nor kill;<br>Wait, watch, and pray. And feast tomorrow noon.”</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
